Reason:
The first statement ignores lines that are less than 3 characters long, which can lead to a situation where a short line is treated as a continuation of the previous line. This is problematic because it can cause unintended behavior in rule processing, as the logic for handling continuations relies on the assumption that lines are of sufficient length to be valid rules. The second and third statements are part of the continuation logic that can incorrectly process these short lines if they are not properly handled.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (len > 0 && line[len - 1] == '\\') {
```
```c
else if (len > 0)
    add_rule(rules, line, filename, filename_off, line_nr);
```
Reason:
The first statement ensures that a line is only treated as a continuation if it is not empty and ends with a backslash, preventing short lines from incorrectly continuing the previous line. The second statement adds the rule only if the line is valid (i.e., has a length greater than 0), ensuring that short lines do not interfere with the rule processing logic. This effectively resolves the issue of short lines being misinterpreted as continuations.

